.TH INVF-MKFS 8 "2026-09" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
invf-mkfs \- create an InvariantFS volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invf-mkfs
.IR image | device
.RI [ size_gb ]
.PP
.B invf-mkfs
.IR dev0 " " size0_gb " " dev1 " " size1_gb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invf-mkfs
formats a file or block device as an InvariantFS volume. InvariantFS is a
semantic, content-addressed filesystem with a bit-exactness invariant: every
compression is verified by decompress-and-compare before it is trusted. See
.BR invarifs (7)
for the format overview.
.PP
With a regular
.I image
file, the volume is
.I size_gb
GiB (default 4, fractional values accepted); the file is sparse. With a block
.IR device ,
the whole device is used unless
.I size_gb
caps it; the device is opened exclusively (locked and dismounted) while
formatting.
.PP
The four-argument form creates a two-device volume (WP25):
.I dev0
is the fast device (metadata + RAW zone + tier arena) and
.I dev1
is the capacity device (metadata mirror + shadow). Single-device images
created by the two-argument form stay byte-identical to the historic layout.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B INVFS_META_FRAC
Scale the metadata zone: the default reserves 1/64 of the volume;
.B INVFS_META_FRAC=16
reserves 1/16 (recommended for churny workloads).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on I/O or capacity errors; 2 on usage errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
invf-mkfs vol.img 10          # 10 GiB sparse image
invf-mkfs /dev/sdb            # whole partition
invf-mkfs fast.img 4 slow.img 32   # two-device volume
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf-fuse (8),
.BR invf-sweep (8),
.BR invf-verify (8),
.BR invarifs (7)
